Top 30 REST API Interview Questions (Java Spring Boot / Microservices)

1. What is REST?

Answer:

REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style for designing web services.
REST APIs use HTTP protocol for communication between client and server.

Interview One-Liner:

REST is an architectural style that uses HTTP methods to perform CRUD operations on resources.
2. What is a Resource in REST?

Answer:

Any object, data, or service exposed through an API.
Identified using a URI.

Example:

/api/customers/101


Interview One-Liner:

A resource is anything that can be uniquely identified by a URI.
3. What are REST Constraints?

Answer:

Client-Server
Stateless
Cacheable
Uniform Interface
Layered System
Code on Demand (Optional)

Interview One-Liner:

REST follows six architectural constraints that ensure scalability and simplicity.
4. What is Statelessness in REST?

Answer:

Server does not store client session information.
Every request contains all required data.

Interview One-Liner:

Each request must be self-contained because REST is stateless.
5. Difference Between REST and SOAP?

REST

Lightweight
Uses JSON/XML
Faster
Uses HTTP

SOAP

Heavyweight
XML only
Slower
Protocol based

Interview One-Liner:

REST is lightweight and flexible, whereas SOAP is a strict protocol.
6. What is URI?

Answer:

Uniform Resource Identifier.
Used to identify resources uniquely.

Example:

/api/accounts/1001


Interview One-Liner:

URI uniquely identifies a REST resource.
7. Difference Between URI and URL?

URI

Identifies resource

URL

Identifies resource + location

Example:

https://bank.com/accounts/1001


Interview One-Liner:

Every URL is a URI, but not every URI is a URL.
8. What are HTTP Methods?
GET
POST
PUT
PATCH
DELETE

Interview One-Liner:

HTTP methods represent different operations on resources.
9. Difference Between GET and POST?

GET

Retrieve data
Idempotent
Parameters in URL

POST

Create resource
Not idempotent
Data in request body

Interview One-Liner:

GET reads data, POST creates data.
10. Difference Between PUT and PATCH?

PUT

Replaces entire resource

PATCH

Updates only specific fields

Example:

PATCH /customers/1
{
  "city":"Mumbai"
}


Interview One-Liner:

PUT performs full update, PATCH performs partial update.
HTTP Status Codes
11. Common HTTP Status Codes

Success

200 OK
201 Created
204 No Content

Client Error

400 Bad Request
401 Unauthorized
403 Forbidden
404 Not Found

Server Error

500 Internal Server Error

Interview One-Liner:

Status codes indicate the outcome of an HTTP request.
12. Difference Between 401 and 403?

401 Unauthorized

Authentication missing or invalid

403 Forbidden

Authentication succeeded
Access denied

Interview One-Liner:

401 means "Who are you?", 403 means "I know you, but you cannot access."
13. When Should You Use 201 Created?

Answer:

After successful resource creation.

Example:

POST /customers


Interview One-Liner:

Use 201 when a new resource is successfully created.
14. When Should You Use 204 No Content?

Answer:

Successful operation with no response body.

Example:

DELETE /accounts/1001


Interview One-Liner:

Use 204 when operation succeeds but nothing needs to be returned.
Request and Response
15. What is Request Body?

Answer:

Data sent by client to server.

Example:

{
  "name":"Parag",
  "city":"Mumbai"
}


Interview One-Liner:

Request body contains data sent from client to server.
16. What is Response Body?

Answer:

Data sent by server to client.

Example:

{
  "id":101,
  "name":"Parag"
}


Interview One-Liner:

Response body contains resource representation returned to client.
17. What are Request Headers?

Examples:

Content-Type
Authorization
Accept

Interview One-Liner:

Headers carry metadata about requests and responses.
18. Difference Between Content-Type and Accept?

Content-Type

Format of request body

Accept

Format expected in response

Example:

Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json


Interview One-Liner:

Content-Type defines what is sent, Accept defines what is expected.
19. What is JSON?

Answer:

JavaScript Object Notation.
Most commonly used REST payload format.

Interview One-Liner:

JSON is lightweight, readable, and language-independent.
20. Why is JSON Preferred Over XML?

JSON

Lightweight
Faster parsing
Less verbose

XML

Verbose
Heavier

Interview One-Liner:

JSON reduces payload size and improves performance.
Security
21. What is Authentication?

Answer:

Verification of user identity.

Examples:

Username/Password
JWT Token
OAuth2

Interview One-Liner:

Authentication answers "Who are you?"
22. What is Authorization?

Answer:

Determines what actions a user can perform.

Interview One-Liner:

Authorization answers "What can you do?"
23. What is JWT?

Answer:

JSON Web Token.
Token-based authentication mechanism.

Structure:

Header.Payload.Signature


Interview One-Liner:

JWT carries user claims and avoids server-side session storage.
24. Why Use HTTPS?

Answer:

Encrypts communication between client and server.

Benefits:

Confidentiality
Integrity
Security

Interview One-Liner:

HTTPS prevents data interception and tampering.
Advanced REST API Concepts
25. What is Idempotency?

Answer:

Multiple identical requests produce the same result.

Idempotent Methods

GET
PUT
DELETE

Non-Idempotent

POST

Interview One-Liner:

Idempotent operations can be safely retried.
26. What is API Versioning?

Examples:

/v1/customers
/v2/customers


Interview One-Liner:

API versioning enables backward compatibility.
27. What is Pagination?

Answer:

Fetch data in chunks instead of all at once.

Example:

/customers?page=0&size=20


Interview One-Liner:

Pagination improves performance for large datasets.
28. What is Rate Limiting?

Answer:

Restricts API request frequency.

Example:

100 requests/minute

Interview One-Liner:

Rate limiting prevents abuse and protects system resources.
29. What is HATEOAS?

Answer:

Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State.
Response contains related links.

Example:

{
  "id":1,
  "name":"Parag",
  "links":[
      "/customers/1/accounts"
  ]
}


Interview One-Liner:

HATEOAS allows API navigation through hypermedia links.
30. How Do You Handle Exceptions in Spring Boot REST APIs?

Using:

@RestControllerAdvice
@ExceptionHandler


Example:

@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<String> handle(Exception ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.internalServerError()
                .body(ex.getMessage());
    }
}


Interview One-Liner:

Global exception handling provides consistent error responses across APIs.
